`,a.jsx(e.h3,{children:"Question"}),`
`,a.jsxs(e.p,{children:["The MFU table above stays at or below ",r(v.bestEager,1),", while one large matrix multiplication reaches ",r(v.ceiling,1)," on the same card. That raises a question about every eager point. How much of a forward goes to the attention kernel the backends compete on, and how much to the small operations between the matrix multiplications? If the small operations took nearly all of it, the backend comparisons above would say little. This section answers the question with a run by the course staff. It is not part of your exercise. Your record has no counterpart to it."]}),`
`,a.jsx(e.h3,{children:"Runs"}),`
`,a.jsxs(e.p,{children:["A staff script, ",a.jsx(e.code,{children:"scripts/staff/w04_compile_profile.py"}),", ran on the same kind of H100 in the same image, with the same batch of ",F.batchSize," × ",s(F.sequenceLength)," tokens. It imports ",a.jsx(e.code,{children:"measure_arm"}),", the FLOP count and the roofline from the gold ",a.jsx(e.code,{children:"compare.py"})," unchanged. For each of the six arms on ",a.jsx("code",{children:F.backend}),", the fastest backend on every arm, it times the model once eager and once under ",a.jsx(e.code,{children:"torch.compile"}),". The eager numbers on this page are the gold points from the section above. The staff run measured them again, and its eager latencies agree with the gold within ",r(d.eagerRerunDrift,1),"."]}),`
`,a.jsxs(e.p,{children:["For one arm, ",a.jsx("code",{children:F.arm}),", the script also records every GPU kernel of ",F.profiledForwards," forwards with the PyTorch profiler, once eager and once compiled, and assigns each kernel to a part of the model. The attention kernel is the one SDPA launches. A matrix multiplication belongs to the MLP, the output head or the attention projections by the shape of its weight. In eager mode, every other kernel belongs to the module that launched it, such as a normalization or the rotary embedding, except the bf16 casts, which are counted on their own. A compiled forward has no modules left, because the compiler fuses operations across module boundaries. Its kernels are assigned by the operations their generated names list: a kernel whose name contains ",a.jsx(e.code,{children:"rsqrt"})," is a normalization, one with ",a.jsx(e.code,{children:"gelu"})," is the MLP activation. A separate loop without the profiler measures the wall time per forward, and a longer one samples the SM clock and the board power every 5 ms."]}),`
`,a.jsx(e.h3,{children:"Plots"}),`
`,a.jsx(te,{src:we,alt:"Two panels. Left: prefill MFU per arm on CUDNN_ATTENTION as paired bars, the compiled bar above the eager bar for all six arms, the dense arms higher than the windowed ones in both modes, and a dashed line for the large matrix multiplication well above every bar. Right: two horizontal stacked bars of milliseconds per mla-dense forward. The eager bar is about half matrix multiplications and the attention kernel, and half normalization, residual additions, rotary embedding and copies, the activation and casts. The compiled bar has about the same matrix multiplications and attention kernel, and much shorter segments for the rest. A black tick on each bar marks the wall time.",caption:"The staff run's figure. Left: prefill MFU per arm, eager from the gold points and compiled from the staff run, with one large matrix multiplication as the dashed line. Right: the GPU kernel time of one mla-dense forward by part, with the wall time per forward as a black tick."}),`
`,a.jsx(e.h3,{children:"Key numbers"}),`
`,a.jsxs(e.p,{children:["Latency per forward and MFU for each arm on ",a.jsx("code",{children:F.backend}),". The eager columns repeat the gold points of the section above."]}),`
`,a.jsx(g,{columns:Ve,rows:Ge}),`
`,a.jsxs(e.p,{children:["One ",a.jsx("code",{children:F.arm})," forward by part. The kernel times and the gaps between kernels come from the profiled forwards. The wall time comes from the separate loop without the profiler, the clock and the power from the longer loop."]}),`
`,a.jsx(g,{columns:Ke,rows:Ye}),`
`,a.jsx(e.h3,{children:"Findings"}),`
`,a.jsxs(e.ul,{children:[`
`,a.jsxs(e.li,{children:["Compilation speeds up every arm by ",l(d.speedUp.min,2)," to ",l(d.speedUp.max,2)," times and lifts the dense arms to ",r(d.denseCompiledMfu.min,1)," to ",r(d.denseCompiledMfu.max,1)," MFU. Every windowed arm stays slower than its dense twin, so the window's slowdown keeps its direction."]}),`
`,a.jsxs(e.li,{children:["The GPU spends almost the whole forward in kernels. Inside the profiled loop, a kernel runs for ",r(d.eagerCoverage)," of the time from the first kernel's start to the last kernel's end eager and ",r(d.compiledCoverage)," compiled, and no two kernels overlap. The rest, ",h(d.eagerGaps)," per eager forward and ",h(d.compiledGaps)," compiled, is time in which the GPU waits for the next kernel. An eager forward runs ",s(d.eagerLaunches)," kernels, ",M(d.eagerSecondsPerKernel)," each on average. The compiled forward runs ",s(d.compiledLaunches),", and most of the time it saves sits in kernels that each read and write a whole activation tensor, not in the gaps."]}),`
`,a.jsxs(e.li,{children:["In the eager forward, the matrix multiplications and the attention kernel take ",h(d.eagerHeavy),", ",r(d.eagerHeavyShare)," of the kernel time. The other ",h(d.eagerRest)," go to normalization, bf16 casts, the rotary embedding, reshapes and copies, residual additions and the activation. These kernels perform no model FLOPs and are limited by memory bandwidth. Compilation shrinks them to ",h(d.compiledRest),". The matrix multiplications and the attention kernel do not get faster. Together they take ",h(d.compiledHeavy)," compiled, ",h(d.heavyExtra)," (",r(d.heavyExtraShare),") more than eager."]}),`
`,a.jsxs(e.li,{children:["Normalization is the largest of these costs in eager mode, ",h(d.eagerNorm),". Autocast runs only the matrix multiplications and attention in bf16. The embedding table is fp32, and adding a bf16 branch output to it keeps the residual stream in fp32, so RMSNorm reads and writes fp32 tensors. Each of the five Linears per layer that read a norm's output, four in attention and one in the MLP, casts that tensor to bf16 on its own. The table counts these with the other bf16 casts, ",h(d.eagerCasts)," in all and most of its last row. The compiled forward fuses each residual addition, normalization and cast into one kernel, ",h(d.compiledNorm)," in all. Its own ",h(d.compiledCasts)," of casts match the time of converting the fp32 weights to bf16 once per forward. The eager timing pays that only once, because autocast keeps the converted weights for its whole context."]}),`
`,a.jsxs(e.li,{children:["The MLP's matrix multiplications run at the card's ceiling: ",r(d.mlpRate)," of the peak, against ",r(d.staffCeiling)," for one large matrix multiplication in the same run. The smaller attention projections reach ",r(d.projectionRate)," and the attention kernel ",r(d.sdpaRate),", counting only its valid pairs. Measured on these kernels alone, the eager ",a.jsx("code",{children:F.arm})," forward would reach about ",r(d.heavyOnlyMfu)," MFU. With the other half of its time it reaches ",r(d.eagerMfu,1),"."]}),`
`,a.jsxs(e.li,{children:["On ",a.jsx("code",{children:F.backend})," the attention kernel takes ",h(d.eagerSdpa),", ",r(d.eagerSdpaShare)," of an eager forward, and a change of backend changes only that term. Differences between backends therefore keep their size in milliseconds but shrink as ratios. On ",a.jsx(e.code,{children:"mha-dense"}),", the forward on ",a.jsx(e.code,{children:"FLASH_ATTENTION"})," takes ",h(d.flashOverCudnn.seconds)," longer than on ",a.jsx(e.code,{children:"CUDNN_ATTENTION"}),", only ",r(d.flashOverCudnn.share)," more. Under compilation the attention kernel, the MLP matrix multiplications and the output head each took ",r(d.compiledSlowdown.min)," to ",r(d.compiledSlowdown.max)," longer than eager, although both modes run the same cuBLAS and cuDNN kernels. Both forwards hold the card at its ",de," W limit, ",s(Math.round(d.eagerPower))," W and ",s(Math.round(d.compiledPower))," W on average. The compiled forward has few memory-bound kernels between its matrix multiplications, so it reaches that limit at a lower SM clock, ",s(Math.round(d.compiledClock))," MHz against ",s(Math.round(d.eagerClock))," MHz eager, ",r(d.clockDrop)," lower. The compute-bound kernels slow down with the clock."]}),`
`]}),`
